Hey, @kylemeenehan.

So this specific case has been a struggle for properly typing MST. Specifically, we do regular type intersection (i.e., `A & B`), and in that case `string & optional(Invariant.A)` will take the last common denominator, which removes the optionality:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/922d66f4-06ad-440a-8898-07e358730513)

I did attempt to fix this in https://github.com/mobxjs/mobx-state-tree/pull/2218, but it unfortunately caused many more "excessively deep" issues and we reverted in https://github.com/mobxjs/mobx-state-tree/pull/2234. My recommendation would be to 🔥 `invariant` from `Base` and only include it in the subtypes, or at least only do that for required params in any base that become optional in the subtype.

At my company, we've done a lot of stuff like this to overcome the issues:

```
const Base = <NameT extends string, ValueT extends string>(name: NameT, invariant: ValueT) => {
  return types.model(name, {
    name: types.string,
    invariant: types.optional(types.literal(invariant), invariant)
  });
}

const TypeA = Base("TypeA", Invariant.A);
const TypeB = Base("TypeB", Invariant.B);
```

I can look into why `TypeA` didn't infer the enum but instead a string, because `string & enum of string values` should be the enum, so _maybe_ there's something we can do there (there are some tricks you can take to avoid "widening" which I can try).

> Thanks for a great library!

❤ we're happy you like it :)

👍 we can clarify in documentation.

And sounds like what you have may be similar to what we did 🙂

```ts
const nodeType = <T extends string>(type: T) => types.optional(types.literal(type), type);
const modelKey = () => nanoid(12);

export const modelType = <Name extends string, Properties extends ModelPropertiesDeclaration = {}>(name: Name, options: Properties) =>
  types
    .model(name, {
      type: nodeType(name),
      key: types.optional(types.identifier, () => modelKey(name)),
      ...options,
    })
    .actions(debugActions);
```

That's our base for pretty much every MST model. We actually use the pattern of base types being functions with generic argument quite a bit, because of the various limitations.

But definitely, if you don't need it in the snapshot, you can likely derive something in a view :)

