Just stick to Promise.then/.catch/.finally instead of try/catch, everything is then clear.

For example
```javascript
    .actions((self) => {
        let abortController = new AbortController();

        // store the Promise chain
        let doThingPromise = Promise.resolve("I am the initial result");

        return {
            doThing: flow(function* doThing() {
                console.log("starting action!");

                // start with Promise.then() and then update the value to the entire chain
                // to ensure that the next action will be proceeded after the current action has completed
                doThingPromise = doThingPromise
                    .then((previousResult) => {
                        console.warn(
                            "[In Promise] starting Promise!",
                            "previous result is [",
                            previousResult,
                            "]"
                        );

                        console.log("[In Promise] fetch is called here");
                        return fetch(
                            "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1",
                            {
                                signal: abortController.signal,
                            }
                        );
                    })
                    .then((response) => {
                        console.log(
                            "[In Promise] after fetch, response is",
                            response
                        );
                        return response.json();
                    })
                    .then((json) => json.title)
                    .catch((error) => {
                        // the error must be handled in this chain
                        // to ensure it will be executed before the next action
                        console.warn(
                            '[In Promise] this should be reached before the second "starting Promise"'
                        );
                        console.error("[In Promise] this failed!", error);

                        // yield here if error
                        // if use Promise.reject, error will be thrown and break the chain
                        // which is not desired
                        return Promise.resolve("I am failed result");
                    });

                // need to identify the value of error result
                const result = yield doThingPromise;
                if (result === "I am failed result") {
                    // handle error value
                } else {
                    self.title = result;
                }
            }),
            cancelThing: () => {
                abortController.abort();

                // update abort controller after current Promise completed
                doThingPromise = doThingPromise.finally(
                    () => (abortController = new AbortController())
                );
            },
        };
    });
```

i ran into a similar issue that i saw in a data intensive app that uses mobx-state-tree that i found.  the full "scenario" that i was seeing was:

1) while scrolling the app, it was unexpectedly slow (there were very noticeably scroll jankyness)
2) when i did performance tracing, i saw it was calling a MST getter (e.g. "get mySlowMethod() {...}") repeatedly during the scrolling stuttering
3) i thought, odd....why is that
4) i added a trace() statement in the getter  (via `import {trace} from 'mobx'`)
5) the trace() statement repeatedly was printing a statement like "'mySlowMethod' was suspended and it will recompute on the next access." during the scrolling stuttering
6) i fixed it by adding a blank autorun that is a blank "usage" of the observer



```typescript
  .views((self) => ({
    get mySlowMethod() {
      /* this is a heavy computation or is slow */
    },
  }))
  .actions((self) => ({
    afterAttach() {
      addDisposer(
        self,
        autorun(() => {
          console.log(self.mySlowMethod); // this simple method of making sure to access self.mySlowMethod keeps the slow getter alive. you can alternatively not console.log it and do something else with it if needed, just make sure to access it in an autorun
        }),
      );
    },
  }));

```


