I have a feature idea that i want to discuss/brainstorm about:

MST "is react but for state", so i want an `modelDidCatch` hook, as react have `componentDidCatch`

I want to give the developer an ability to recover/"ignore" runtime type errors in the level of the model that had the issue (and not the applySnapshot root/create)
This is a code that +- do what i want:

https://github.com/Bnaya/mst-error-boundaries/blob/0ca934c4832651bc75bdee8f3906fb922906f531/src/mst.ts#L3-L24
```ts
export function addMSTErrorBoundary<IT extends IAnyModelType>(
  Model: IT,
  /**
   * Supply a valid snapshot to use, instead of the failing one
   * We also give you the validation error of the snapshot, and the bad snapshot.
   */
  getFallbackSnapshot: (
    error: unknown,
    originalButBadSnapshot: unknown
  ) => IT["CreationType"]
) {
  return types.snapshotProcessor(Model, {
    preProcessor(snMaybeBad: IT["CreationType"]): IT["CreationType"] {
      try {
        typecheck(Model, snMaybeBad);
        return snMaybeBad;
      } catch (e) {
        return getFallbackSnapshot(e, snMaybeBad);
      }
    }
  });
}
```

**Motivation:**
From my experience and others
On dev time (But in some cases even on prod time),
We get external data that's not 100% compatible with our models.
So we get an error, and we then dig in the error, finding the miss-behaving snapshot, fix the data source (eg server endpoint) or change you model, reload, and hope we fixed it.
In some cases, the data source is not even in our immediate control, so we write a code (Maybe inside preprocess snapshot) that tries to fix the snapshot, even with hard coded data/default value just to get going.

But in cases that its a very big snapshot, for a nested model,
For example "CategoryModel" -> "BrandModel" -> creatives[0-1000] 
One bad creative snapshot can send you digging in very long exception and/or writing code just to find/filter out the bad creative do you could keep working and not handling that problem immediately.

The above code, lets you selectively wrap models in your tree and if it fails to create of them, you can return a good snapshot instead (and put a warning of the console/server).
The "good snapshot" can be a snapshot that indicates a local error state (if the model has one) or just default placeholder data. thing about it as `componentDidCatch`

That feature might fit as external utility/recommended pattern or even something inside the library.

To see the code in action, there are tests that shows the some basic use cases:
https://github.com/Bnaya/mst-error-boundaries#example-how-to-make-errors-boundaries-like-mechanism-in-mst

