The middleware is a transaction tracking implementation. It allows us to build transactions that record model property changes over a series of actions (include async flow actions), building up a collection of snapshots of model changes which can then be all 'commited' at once to a backend. So it makes use of `onResume` to swap out the transactions that are being written to as asynchronous actions are paused/resumed.

We've tested out the suggested fix, which is to change the tracking middleware v1 to only delete the `rootId` if the `rootId` and the `parentId` of the call are the same. Seems to work. But if middleware v1 is going to be completely deprecated then I think middleware v2 needs to support a resume hook similar to the existing middleware for our use-case.

That sounds doable, since it is pretty close to what atomic does.

The trick is in the first part of the "onStart" part, inside the recorder filter function, which makes sure it won't record patches outside of the flow / flow children actions (or sync actions, it is all the same for the new actionTrackingMiddleware)

```ts
const atomic = createActionTrackingMiddleware2<{ recorder: IPatchRecorder }>({
    filter(call) {
        // only call the methods below for actions that were not being recorded,
        // but do not call them for child acions (which inherit a copy of the env)
        if (call.env) {
            // already recording
            return false
        }

        return true
    },
    onStart(call) {
        const recorder = recordPatches(call.tree, (_patch, _inversePatch, actionContext) => {
            /////// the trick is here ///////////
            // only record patches that were generated by this action or children of this action
            return !!actionContext && isActionContextThisOrChildOf(actionContext, call.id)
        })
        recorder.resume()
        call.env = {
            recorder
        }
    },
    onFinish(call, error) {
        const recorder = call.env!.recorder
        call.env = undefined
        recorder.stop()

        /////////////////// here you would send recorder patches to backend //////////////////
        // might event want to only send them if there was no error (exception thrown on the action), and to undo if there was an error
        /*if (error !== undefined) {
            recorder.undo()
        }*/
    }
})
```

