body:
## Problem

MST is known to be much less performant in terms of both CPU load and memory footprint than pure mobx. Currently the problem is partly solved by making everything as lazy as it can be.
But the problem reappears as soon as one needs to aggregate some data across all the instances. 

For the example of 50 Parents (4 props + children, 2 computeds, 4 actions) each holding 100 Children (2 props + children, 2 computeds, 2 actions) each holding 10 GrandChildren (2 props, 2 computeds, 1 action) - resulting in 60100 ObjectNodes + 110200 ScalarNodes:
 - creation is **850ms** and full initialization is **3900ms** (resulting in **4750ms**) vs **1550ms** for the same setup in pure mobx - **3x** difference;
 - memory footprint is roughly **150Mb** vs **60Mb** for the same setup in pure mobx - **2.5x** difference.

There is no doubt that MST provides a good layer of convinience by adding strong typings (even runtime), bridge to immutable objects (snapshots/patches), middlewares, hooks and so on. And those benefits could not come without a cost. But we could try to put some efforts to minimize this cost.

## Proposal

Although there are several ways to reduce overhead, the most efficient for now is to move ```actions``` from instance to prototype. The most significant impact of this cahnge is that there will be no ```self``` within ```actions``` - the code inside actions must use ```this``` instead. 
I've made a proof of concept test withing my current project (> 40 different trees, with references, flows, middlewares etc.) and didn't meet a single issue with incorrectly bound ```this```. Nevertheless, end-users will have to run a codemod or make by-hand codebase changes to swich from ```self``` to ```this``` - thats why I propose to release it under separate type (SelflessModel, FastModel, BoundModel - name to be discussed). As soon as we get positive feedback from users and eliminate possible bugs we can make this implementation default.

For consistency purpose we should also allow use ```this``` withinf ```views```/```volatile```/```extend```. We actually do for ```views```, but do not encourage for now.

## Motivation

For my proof of concept implementation I had the following results:
- creation is **880ms** and initialization is **3000ms** (resulting in **3880ms**) - **~18%** faster;
- memory footprint is roughly **120Mb** - **~20%** less.

## Detailed design

Overview of proposed changes has the following look:
- new type has the same interface as existing ```Model```;
- ```actions```/```views```/```volatile```/```extend``` accept plain objects or ```() => PLAIN_OBJECT``` delegates, which use ```this``` inside;
```js
const MyFastModel = FastModel.create({
    foo: types.string,
    bar: types.number
  })
  .views({
    get bar2x() {
      return this.bar * 2;
    }
  })
  .views(() => ({
    get foo2x() {
      return `${this.foo} ${this.foo}`;
    }
  }))
  .actions({
    setFoo(val) {
      this.foo = val;
    }
  })
  .actions(() => ({
    setBar(val) {
      this.bar = val;
    }
  }))
```
- all the methods above just accumulate passed keys within internal objects (i.e. ```_actionDescriptors```, ```_viewDescriptors``` via ```cloneAndEnhance```);
- upon first ```instantiate``` call a base object is constructed which holds all the actions wrapped in ```createActionInvoker``` (which is currently called for every action on every instance) - this base object is used as prototype for ```observableInstance```
- ```createActionInvoker``` is slightly modified to use ```this``` instead of ```target``` as ```context.context```; this one could be further refactored, but I'll describe it at separete RFC later on;
- ```ObjectNode```'s ```_applyPatches```/```_applySnapshot``` are extracted from ```preboot``` and just bound at this point to ```storedValue``` (which is cheaper and fater, than recreating);

## Drawbacks

- ```self``` is positioned as one of the MST benefits, but this proposal discouragaes ```self```;
- I'm not sure about the impact of such changes on typesystem of MST; @xaviergonz, could you please tell if it's ok?

I'm willing to make PR, if the idea is accepted. 
I'll also try to describe other performance-related RFCs withing a week or two.

